{
  "description": "MUST-reject vectors. Each case starts from a valid construction (the seeds of key-wrap.json and ed25519.json, and the items of manifest.json, commit.json, and federation.json) and applies exactly one mutation; a conformant implementation MUST reject it. 'op' is the operation: 'key-unwrap' (X25519-HKDF-SHA256-AESGCM-v1, info avp/rdk-wrap/v1) and 'wrap-unwrap' (the same scheme under the case's `info`) MUST fail authentication; 'ed25519-verify' MUST return false, and its last five cases are the strict verification rejects of SPEC 2 (a small-order or non-canonical public key or R, a non-canonical S), which carry no base; 'manifest-verify' (ownerSignature over the state bytes S under ownerKey, SPEC 4.2) MUST fail; 'manifest-link' MUST find that `next` does not link to `prev` (manifestSeq + 1 and prevManifestHash = manifestHash(prev), SPEC 6.3) although `next` is validly signed; 'commit-verify' (commitId recomputes from the header and authorSignature verifies, SPEC 7.1) MUST fail; 'payload-decrypt' (AES-256-GCM under dataKeyHex with AAD v2 built from `aad`, SPEC 4.3) MUST fail authentication; 'acceptance-verify' (the signature under verifyHostKey, SPEC 11.3) and 'leave-verify' (the signature under memberKey, SPEC 11.4) MUST return false. Where a case has `base`, replacing its fields with `base` gives the valid construction, which MUST be accepted. Wire base64 strictness is implementation-specific and intentionally not tested here. Generated by vectors/generate.ts from the same seeds as the positive vectors.",
